  "summary": "Scope Lighthouse's SIT access-control header to same-origin requests only",
  "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/issue/19429",
  "description": "**RESOLVED — accepted, not fixing. See \"Resolution\" at the bottom.**\n\n**Context**\n\nFlagged by CodeRabbit on PR #80, verified as a real, currently-live gap. `lighthouserc.js`'s `collect.settings.extraHeaders` forwards the SIT MRT gate secret (`LHCI_SFDC_ACCESS_CONTROL`, header `x-sfdc-access-control`) via Puppeteer/Lighthouse's `Page.setExtraHTTPHeaders` (Chrome DevTools Protocol) — which applies the header to *every* network request the page makes during the audit, not just requests to the SIT origin. The homepage loads resources from many cross-origin third parties (Stripe, PayPal, Adyen, Measmerize, Google Maps/Pay, `images.ctfassets.net`, etc.) — every one of those receives the SIT access-control credential in its request headers today.\n\n**Investigation summary (2026-09-01) — every fix path ruled out**\n\n1. Secret leaking into the uploaded `.lighthouseci/` GitHub Actions artifact — *fixed* separately in PR #80 (redaction step, fails closed on error). Not affected by this closure.\n1. Puppeteer request interception, scoped to SIT origin only — breaks Lighthouse's own DevTools protocol session (confirmed via real `lhci collect` runs against live SIT: `PROTOCOL_TIMEOUT`, fatal `CSS.enable` timeout). Matches a long-standing, still-open upstream bug, `GoogleChrome/lighthouse-ci#789` (open since 2022, no maintainer fix, another user hit the identical use case independently). A dependency bump was investigated and not recommended — no evidence a newer `@lhci/cli`/`puppeteer-core` resolves this specific conflict.\n1. Cookie via `page.setCookie()` (Lighthouse's own recommended pattern for authenticated pages) — confirmed via plain `curl` (no Puppeteer involved) that the SIT MRT gateway's access control does not accept cookies at all, only the header. An earlier report that this worked was a false positive from CDN edge caching and was corrected.\n1. MRT-native alternatives, per official Salesforce docs (`developer.salesforce.com/docs/commerce/pwa-kit-managed-runtime/guide/managed-runtime-administration.html` and the linked Access Control Headers reference): MRT access control supports only (a) up to 2 custom headers, or (b) an IP allowlist capped at 250 addresses. No cookie support exists on the platform at all. The IP allowlist path was checked against GitHub Actions' published runner ranges (`api.github.com/meta`) — **7,251 CIDR blocks** for the standard `ubuntu-latest` shared runner pool this workflow uses, ~29x over the 250-entry cap. Not feasible without switching to GitHub's paid static-IP larger runners, which wasn't pursued.\n1. `blockedUrlPatterns` (block the third-party origins from loading during the Lighthouse audit entirely, so there's nothing for the header to leak to) — identified as technically viable but not implemented, because it would change what the audit measures (real third-party script impact on performance/best-practices would no longer be reflected).\n\n**Resolution (2026-09-01): accepted, not fixing.** `x-sfdc-access-control` gates access to lower/non-production environments only (SIT and similar preview tiers) — it is never present in production. The credential leaking to third-party request headers during a CI Lighthouse run therefore doesn't expose anything beyond access to a non-production preview environment, which is a bounded, acceptable risk. No further engineering time will be spent on this. If this constraint ever changes (e.g. the same access-control mechanism gets reused for a production-adjacent tier), revisit this decision — do not assume it stays low-risk forever.\n\nNote: Jira workflow transitions available to this issue from `To Do` were only `Blocked` / `Ready for Refinement` — no direct won't-fix/Done transition was available via the API. Status left as `To Do` with this resolution documented; close it manually in the Jira UI if a transition is available there that wasn't exposed here.",
